WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:03.780
There's a new role popping up inside some of the fastest growing startups and

00:03.780 --> 00:07.280
it's spreading quickly across the industry. In the last year alone, job

00:07.280 --> 00:12.880
postings for this role have surged over 5,000 percent and it's called a go-to

00:12.880 --> 00:18.640
market engineer. But what the — is a go-to-market engineer? Is it engineering?

00:18.880 --> 00:23.480
Is it marketing? Is it sales? Is it an internal facing role or an external role?

00:23.620 --> 00:28.200
What do they actually do every day? I have no idea. Which is exactly why I

00:28.200 --> 00:33.480
ended up here inside Clay. Because they didn't just adopt this role, they actually

00:33.480 --> 00:37.320
coined it. Clay was founded in 2017 and in just a few years it went from a small

00:37.320 --> 00:40.920
product to powering go-to-market teams at some of the largest companies in the

00:40.920 --> 00:46.520
world like Anthropic and Google. It's now valued at five billion dollars. And this

00:46.520 --> 00:49.760
whole idea of go-to-market engineering seems to be a big part of how they've

00:49.760 --> 00:53.340
scaled so fast. So I'm meeting up with Everett Berry, their head of go-to-market

00:53.340 --> 00:57.280
engineering, to find out what it is, what they actually do all day, and how it's

00:57.280 --> 01:03.580
driving Clay's growth. But first, in order for this to be a real full day, we got to go to Jersey.

01:10.260 --> 01:11.340
He's on the phone.

01:12.360 --> 01:13.580
He's working already.

01:14.160 --> 01:15.460
And then just...

01:15.460 --> 01:16.620
And by the way...

01:18.900 --> 01:19.640
What's up?

01:20.740 --> 01:21.340
How's it going?

01:21.580 --> 01:27.260
Good. And then yeah, on the train, we got to write a LinkedIn post and then we got to like finish with this

01:27.260 --> 01:28.040
enablement session.

01:28.180 --> 01:28.780
Oh gosh.

01:29.000 --> 01:31.540
Leaving the train. Please watch the gap.

01:32.360 --> 01:38.200
The trick with this commute is basically the cell service goes in and out. So I got to get all my stuff like ready to go.

01:38.420 --> 01:39.780
You've mastered your commute.

01:39.900 --> 01:40.440
Yeah. Yeah.

01:40.840 --> 01:49.680
All right. So we're basically racing the clock. 45 minute train ride, spotty service, and somehow the goal is to write a full LinkedIn post and prep for a presentation he had later that day.

01:49.680 --> 01:53.300
We're going to do a look at how Clay's like internal GTM engineering team works.

01:53.500 --> 01:55.520
Are you under pressure because we're watching you?

01:55.920 --> 01:58.000
No. No. I feel comfortable.

01:58.220 --> 01:58.460
Okay. Okay. Good.

01:58.740 --> 02:01.540
People are like, what are your goals at Clay? I'm like, I'm trying to become famous.

02:02.460 --> 02:03.640
Smile at the camera really quick.

02:05.100 --> 02:06.240
Make Everett famous.

02:08.240 --> 02:09.820
I said, I think we're ready to post.

02:09.820 --> 02:10.380
Let's go.

02:11.400 --> 02:12.200
Post made.

02:12.640 --> 02:13.480
What should I comment?

02:13.840 --> 02:15.120
Comment, "So insightful."

02:15.400 --> 02:15.700
Perfect.

02:17.880 --> 02:21.120
Okay. Yeah. So we just got to, and then we're there.

02:21.540 --> 02:21.640
Cool.

02:22.480 --> 02:24.380
So Everett's already in a meeting.

02:24.900 --> 02:27.080
To like make him aware of that too, he may just not know.

02:27.460 --> 02:29.640
I would do, I'd probably do an intro for this one.

02:29.780 --> 02:33.280
The second we got to the office, Everett's dream of becoming famous started to come true.

02:33.480 --> 02:34.340
Okay. Welcome to the office.

02:34.340 --> 02:35.100
Thank you.

02:35.400 --> 02:37.480
His coworkers stopped to take photos of him.

02:37.600 --> 02:39.120
I think it's because they thought it was funny.

02:39.260 --> 02:40.780
He was being followed around by a camera crew.

02:41.160 --> 02:42.020
What's going on?

02:42.280 --> 02:42.480
Great.

02:43.100 --> 02:44.120
Nice to meet you guys.

02:44.620 --> 02:47.620
Anyway, he got straight to business and jumped into a meeting with Zach.

02:47.840 --> 02:48.680
And I'm not going to lie.

02:48.840 --> 02:52.240
Within about 30 seconds, I had no idea what they were talking about.

02:52.300 --> 02:55.060
So I left and decided to go explore the office.

02:55.320 --> 02:56.940
Just came across a cinnamon roll.

02:57.260 --> 02:58.380
I kind of want to bite.

03:04.620 --> 03:05.740
Are you busy right now?

03:06.500 --> 03:08.220
Can we ask you a couple questions?

03:08.580 --> 03:08.820
Sure.

03:09.080 --> 03:09.540
What do you do?

03:09.800 --> 03:11.260
I do go-to-market engineering here.

03:11.440 --> 03:12.700
I am a go-to-market engineer.

03:13.020 --> 03:14.620
I am an internal go-to-market engineer.

03:14.960 --> 03:16.380
What the heck is a go-to-market engineer?

03:16.480 --> 03:17.880
What the heck is a go-to-market engineer?

03:18.200 --> 03:18.540
Sorry.

03:18.740 --> 03:22.100
So this is why I think interviewing Clay about go-to-market engineering can be confusing.

03:22.400 --> 03:23.620
I am a go-to-market engineer.

03:23.620 --> 03:26.120
I am the core user of every other company.

03:26.460 --> 03:26.500
Yeah.

03:26.500 --> 03:27.860
We try to sell to people like me.

03:28.160 --> 03:28.840
And he was right.

03:29.200 --> 03:30.960
It is confusing.

03:31.280 --> 03:34.340
Trying to explain this in a video, I think you guys will have a lot of trouble.

03:34.620 --> 03:36.460
Well, that wasn't very comforting.

03:36.700 --> 03:41.800
But I figured if I could understand what Spencer does, I could figure out how Clay works.

03:42.200 --> 03:46.280
And that's when he started talking about pickles.

03:46.760 --> 03:49.680
I default to the pickle analogy, which is imagine you make pickles.

03:49.880 --> 03:50.900
I've never had a pickle.

03:51.160 --> 03:52.240
You've never had a pickle?

03:52.240 --> 03:53.480
That's heinous.

03:53.620 --> 03:54.980
They look so gross.

03:55.400 --> 03:58.180
The main thing we're thinking is how do you grow your business?

03:58.500 --> 04:02.220
You need to think, who are my customers and how can I find more of them?

04:02.440 --> 04:06.900
Somebody making pickles in New York City needs to build a list of all of the delis in New York

04:06.900 --> 04:09.280
and say which ones are my customers, which ones are not.

04:09.520 --> 04:10.880
Now I know the ones that I should sell to.

04:11.360 --> 04:16.040
Or maybe even for the customers, maybe I want to expand my customer into different pickle varieties, right?

04:16.220 --> 04:18.760
And we know exactly which ones they're buying and which ones they're not buying.

04:19.420 --> 04:20.500
We build that list.

04:20.700 --> 04:21.960
We find the right people.

04:22.240 --> 04:23.980
We find the decision maker.

04:24.180 --> 04:25.520
Who's the most likely to buy the pickle?

04:25.820 --> 04:27.480
And then we sell you pickles.

04:27.820 --> 04:31.340
So what Spencer is describing is the core job of a go-to-market engineer.

04:31.640 --> 04:38.660
They take messy data about who's out there, track signs of interest, which is something like who visits your website, and analyze buying behavior.

04:38.660 --> 04:44.580
Then they turn it into a system that tells you who to sell to, who's most likely to buy, and how to reach them.

04:44.840 --> 04:49.500
And instead of doing all of that manually, like teams used to, they automate the entire thing.

04:49.800 --> 04:53.180
Clay is the tool that lets go-to-market engineers build those systems.

04:53.540 --> 04:56.940
And when I asked Spencer what else he's been up to, he said...

04:56.940 --> 04:59.100
It might be because I'm in a good mood, but I've been hyping people up.

05:00.980 --> 05:01.340
Emily?

05:01.580 --> 05:02.400
Emily's actually...

05:02.400 --> 05:05.640
She knows exactly how to target and who to target at any given moment.

05:05.920 --> 05:06.500
Thank you.

05:07.500 --> 05:08.520
This is not good hype.

05:08.640 --> 05:09.440
We've got to find better hype.

05:09.960 --> 05:11.620
Alright, I'm going to go hype up somebody else.

05:12.120 --> 05:18.660
People, engineers have like onboarding projects to learn the code base, and Jenny's onboarding project still gives me a smile once a week.

05:19.080 --> 05:21.280
I think Spencer's just the best hype man.

05:21.580 --> 05:22.860
Like, Spencer brings the best energy to the office.

05:22.860 --> 05:23.900
I'm a personality hype man.

05:24.340 --> 05:25.420
That was slightly awkward.

05:25.680 --> 05:27.800
Then it was time for me to go meet back up with Everett.

05:28.060 --> 05:29.060
Alright, quick pause.

05:29.480 --> 05:31.800
If you stayed at the end of this video, I'm going to try a pickle.

05:32.100 --> 05:37.240
But also, I wanted to let you know that if you're trying to wrap your head around this whole go-to-market engineering thing, don't worry.

05:37.440 --> 05:38.160
I got you.

05:38.300 --> 05:47.280
We put together a full playbook breaking down how Clay built this into a $5 billion growth engine, what the role actually is, and how to structure it inside your own company.

05:47.280 --> 05:53.980
Because right now, this is one of the fastest growing roles in B2B, and most companies don't know how to hire for it or use it.

05:54.100 --> 05:57.100
So, if you want the playbook, it's free. Link in the description.

05:57.500 --> 05:58.840
Alright, back to Clay.

05:59.040 --> 06:02.580
On my way back downstairs, I ran into Varun, one of Clay's co-founders.

06:02.720 --> 06:05.680
So, I asked if he could walk me through how Clay got here.

06:06.040 --> 06:06.320
Hi.

06:06.920 --> 06:10.920
What I didn't realize was that Clay's growth was pretty slow in the beginning.

06:11.200 --> 06:15.020
For the first five years, they made basically zero dollars in revenue.

06:15.760 --> 06:16.980
Nothing really happened for a while.

06:16.980 --> 06:19.560
But eventually, they landed on an idea.

06:19.920 --> 06:24.220
What if a spreadsheet could actually pull data from anywhere on the internet?

06:24.500 --> 06:31.740
And then in like February of 2022, we launched the product on Product Hunt.

06:31.940 --> 06:33.420
And that's when they started noticing something.

06:33.640 --> 06:40.160
The people who really understood the product were technical scrappy operators using it to build systems to find and reach customers.

06:40.160 --> 06:43.900
And watching how those users worked gave Clay a clear direction.

06:44.160 --> 06:50.760
Instead of just being a data tool, Clay became a system that lets teams find the right people, understand them, and reach out to them.

06:51.060 --> 06:55.020
Then, in late 2022, artificial intelligence supercharged all of it.

06:55.020 --> 07:01.640
Now, instead of just finding the right people, teams could actually generate personalized outreach automatically, all in Clay.

07:01.920 --> 07:04.400
And that's when startups started using it.

07:04.700 --> 07:08.740
I still remember, by the way, Rippling was a pretty meaningful customer for us.

07:08.860 --> 07:10.380
They signed in March of 2023.

07:10.740 --> 07:12.480
They were the customer that pushed us over a million dollars.

07:12.480 --> 07:15.540
But then, heading into 2024, they hit a new challenge.

07:15.940 --> 07:18.580
Startups were using Clay, but enterprise companies weren't.

07:18.840 --> 07:21.180
Well, how do we become like an amazing company?

07:21.360 --> 07:23.800
Well, you kind of need other amazing companies to use your product.

07:23.980 --> 07:27.340
And it just so happens that other amazing big companies don't want to use your product.

07:27.560 --> 07:29.000
If, like, we need to kind of help them.

07:29.180 --> 07:30.240
So, like, okay, how do we do that?

07:30.360 --> 07:31.640
Well, apparently that's what sales is.

07:31.880 --> 07:33.760
But Varun didn't want traditional salespeople.

07:33.960 --> 07:35.020
The product was too complex.

07:35.280 --> 07:38.540
So, he looked at Clay's users for people who already knew how to use it.

07:38.640 --> 07:39.980
Everett was there in our community.

07:40.360 --> 07:42.240
So, you were using Clay before you joined Clay?

07:42.240 --> 07:42.720
Yes.

07:43.140 --> 07:43.360
Okay.

07:43.840 --> 07:45.900
And I was like, hey, what if you did sales?

07:46.620 --> 07:47.580
And you're like, what?

07:48.180 --> 07:49.180
I've never done sales before.

07:49.300 --> 07:50.100
What was your reaction?

07:50.500 --> 07:53.700
I was like, I'm, first of all, more of an engineer.

07:53.860 --> 07:56.100
Second of all, more of a, like, a growth person.

07:56.260 --> 07:57.180
Sales is like, icky.

07:57.400 --> 07:58.140
Yeah, sales is icky.

07:58.320 --> 08:00.200
But that's exactly why Varun wanted him.

08:00.440 --> 08:05.100
He was someone that could build systems and could show another company how to grow using Clay.

08:05.360 --> 08:08.180
At one point I was like, so, Varun, like, should I be, like, head of sales?

08:08.400 --> 08:09.080
And he was like, no.

08:09.480 --> 08:11.160
He was like, GTM engineering is the thing.

08:11.160 --> 08:13.480
And just like that, the team had a name.

08:13.640 --> 08:16.360
So, Everett joined, helping bring Clay into enterprise companies.

08:16.660 --> 08:19.940
And then I didn't sell anything at Clay for six months.

08:20.200 --> 08:22.120
But eventually, he started to figure it out.

08:22.220 --> 08:28.680
Instead of just telling companies what the product could do, the go-to-market engineers started building real workflows for each prospect.

08:28.680 --> 08:30.600
Well, no one liked their data stack.

08:30.720 --> 08:31.820
Everyone thought it was too expensive.

08:32.000 --> 08:32.940
The quality wasn't good enough.

08:33.060 --> 08:34.080
There was too many vendors to manage.

08:34.240 --> 08:35.660
So, like, hey, we'll solve that for you.

08:35.820 --> 08:38.220
We'll prove to you that we can solve it through this data test.

08:38.440 --> 08:40.520
Let's start with that use case, get you on that.

08:40.660 --> 08:48.380
And then we can expand you to all these other use cases, outbound, inbound, CRM, expansion, all these other things that are ABM that we can do for you after the fact.

08:48.380 --> 08:52.300
And by the end of the year, they were landing companies like Figma and OpenAI.

08:52.580 --> 08:56.600
By 2025, they scaled that approach and crossed $100 million in revenue.

08:56.840 --> 09:00.860
Today, they have over 16,000 customers, including hundreds of enterprise teams.

09:01.080 --> 09:05.060
And a big part of how they got here came from building their go-to-market engineering team.

09:05.300 --> 09:10.380
Okay, so now that I know why Clay created go-to-market engineers, it was time to see what they do all day.

09:10.380 --> 09:14.560
Alright, so we are in Boomi. What up?

09:15.400 --> 09:15.740
Hi.

09:15.980 --> 09:16.600
Hi, how are you guys?

09:16.740 --> 09:17.320
I'm Payton.

09:17.560 --> 09:17.980
Nice to meet you.

09:18.520 --> 09:21.120
That's Daniel, and that's Melina.

09:21.420 --> 09:27.860
So then I just wanted to kind of quickly go over what is present in the GTME training around pricing from Zona.

09:28.140 --> 09:29.380
Who is this deck for?

09:29.780 --> 09:30.640
The GTMEs.

09:31.580 --> 09:32.060
Of course.

09:33.180 --> 09:34.240
Alright, awesome.

09:35.260 --> 09:39.880
And then we had so many meetings.

09:39.880 --> 09:42.360
Well, in case you haven't noticed, I brought a camera crew with me today.

09:42.520 --> 09:43.200
Oh, I bet you did.

09:43.640 --> 09:47.280
It got to the point where Everett just kept disappearing into so many meetings.

09:47.520 --> 09:49.200
And I kept losing him.

09:53.260 --> 09:54.800
Well, we lost Everett.

09:55.180 --> 09:57.920
He's just such a busy guy, so now we gotta go find him.

09:59.040 --> 10:00.480
See where he possibly could have went.

10:00.840 --> 10:01.880
Everett, where are you?

10:02.340 --> 10:03.040
Did he leave?

10:03.720 --> 10:05.880
We saw him walk past us.

10:06.860 --> 10:08.520
No one responded.

10:09.880 --> 10:12.940
It's the problem with these operators.

10:13.400 --> 10:14.240
They're just so busy.

10:14.840 --> 10:16.200
Yeah, I lost you for a while.

10:16.220 --> 10:19.200
I know, I was, well, I had a meeting with Becca there.

10:19.400 --> 10:19.880
Oh, okay.

10:20.020 --> 10:20.240
So.

10:20.620 --> 10:22.900
Not gonna lie, I was getting really hungry.

10:23.740 --> 10:27.900
And just when I was about to go get food, Everett said we had to take a field trip to the park.

10:29.180 --> 10:30.960
Oh, yeah, I was wondering what was gonna happen.

10:30.960 --> 10:34.640
So are customer meetings outside the office not the usual?

10:35.000 --> 10:35.460
No, they are.

10:35.680 --> 10:36.240
Okay, okay.

10:36.700 --> 10:36.880
Yeah.

10:37.180 --> 10:39.180
And a lot of times we go to their office instead of like walking around.

10:39.180 --> 10:39.420
Right.

10:40.340 --> 10:40.700
Hi.

10:41.760 --> 10:42.520
How are you?

10:42.580 --> 10:42.900
Good.

10:43.060 --> 10:43.800
Good to see you.

10:43.880 --> 10:44.320
You too.

10:44.540 --> 10:44.600
Hi.

10:45.560 --> 10:47.380
Nice to meet you.

10:48.160 --> 10:49.700
All right, shall we stroll around the park?

10:49.780 --> 10:50.380
Yeah, let's do it.

10:50.380 --> 10:52.640
This was a customer meeting with someone from AWS.

10:52.720 --> 10:56.380
A chance to check in, see how things were going and keep building the relationship.

10:57.180 --> 10:59.640
This is a customer meeting everybody.

11:00.540 --> 11:03.420
And I, not allowed in that conversation.

11:12.300 --> 11:13.760
Looks like it's going well.

11:14.020 --> 11:14.720
How'd it go?

11:15.020 --> 11:15.440
Great.

11:15.820 --> 11:16.220
Amazing.

11:16.720 --> 11:16.940
All right.

11:17.460 --> 11:17.620
See you.

11:17.620 --> 11:17.800
Bye.

11:19.060 --> 11:23.880
I've been seeing a lot of meetings and on the walk back, I wanted to ask Everett a bit more about what his team does.

11:24.120 --> 11:33.820
And so what we do is go deep with customers and discover all their use cases and like really carefully kind of build like a POC for them.

11:34.060 --> 11:36.740
There were three things his team needs to be able to do.

11:37.060 --> 11:41.000
Know go-to-market well, know Clay well, and basically be a good salesperson.

11:41.220 --> 11:44.720
When we got back to the office, Everett had more meetings, of course.

11:44.720 --> 11:47.980
So I took the opportunity to go chat with the rest of the team.

11:48.600 --> 12:00.340
I think so go-to-market engineering for as like a whole, I think is really kind of just very intertwined with Clay's growth as a company because we are the kind of pioneers of the go-to-market engineer.

12:00.340 --> 12:04.660
I ended up talking to George for a very long time.

12:05.100 --> 12:08.040
I'm talking to 10 different go-to-market orgs a day.

12:08.300 --> 12:12.080
And so a lot of times when they're creating a role, it's to solve the problem that they have.

12:12.320 --> 12:16.020
So you want an application of Clay for these customers.

12:16.320 --> 12:18.640
So why not just call yourself a salesperson?

12:18.960 --> 12:19.660
Good question.

12:20.160 --> 12:23.000
And then he said something that stopped me in my tracks.

12:23.000 --> 12:26.140
So there's two go-to-market engineering teams.

12:26.340 --> 12:27.580
Wait, two teams?

12:27.940 --> 12:29.280
I needed to find Everett.

12:31.580 --> 12:34.420
GTM engineers use technology to help businesses grow.

12:34.920 --> 12:35.480
Okay.

12:37.040 --> 12:41.100
So there are two GTME teams at Clay.

12:41.400 --> 12:43.020
The first team is the internal team.

12:43.200 --> 12:46.040
Other companies have only this for GTM engineering.

12:46.200 --> 12:48.360
They have internal people focused on pipeline generation.

12:48.680 --> 12:50.800
They're not traditional ops or marketing people.

12:50.800 --> 12:56.100
They're engineers, but instead of building software, they're building systems that power a company's revenue.

12:56.280 --> 13:00.280
Things like pipeline operations, automated follow-ups, and content generation.

13:00.620 --> 13:06.460
They're basically helping sort through the best prospects to target.

13:06.600 --> 13:06.700
Right.

13:06.920 --> 13:07.060
Correct.

13:07.240 --> 13:07.560
Okay.

13:07.720 --> 13:09.080
But I still had one question.

13:09.280 --> 13:14.140
If most companies only have the internal team, how does Clay's second team actually fit in?

13:14.220 --> 13:15.440
And how do they work together?

13:15.660 --> 13:17.740
So I had Everett map it out.

13:17.740 --> 13:21.880
Basically, the internal team is responsible for bringing in all different types of leads.

13:22.160 --> 13:28.880
Enterprise customers or potential ones go to our second GTME team for deployed GTMEs.

13:29.740 --> 13:31.540
That's Everett's team, the salespeople.

13:32.000 --> 13:34.420
And this is where Clay did something most companies haven't.

13:34.500 --> 13:39.500
They took people with the same skills as the internal team and deployed them directly to customers.

13:39.500 --> 13:44.140
So this GTME team receives what this GTME team kind of creates.

13:44.360 --> 13:46.640
The internal team generates and qualifies leads.

13:46.860 --> 13:51.280
And when they identify something that seems like a strong enterprise fit, it gets passed to Everett's team.

13:51.480 --> 13:57.360
And so, you know, we're basically coming in almost as like a go-to-market consultant and figuring out,

13:57.360 --> 14:02.340
Hey, what is the like root cause of problems you're facing in the go-to-market, you know, stack?

14:03.280 --> 14:04.380
And how do we fix that?

14:04.480 --> 14:06.500
And how can we build something in Clay that will solve that problem?

14:06.660 --> 14:08.080
It's completely custom to the customer.

14:08.480 --> 14:15.120
So we're doing these kind of one-of-one builds for people pre-sale to just show them like how they would run this,

14:15.600 --> 14:23.260
you know, use case in Clay for like a small sample of their, you know, their customers or their data that they're trying to source.

14:23.260 --> 14:26.740
They are people with the same skills pretty much divided depending on personality.

14:26.960 --> 14:30.200
The more engineering minded ones gravitate towards internal infrastructure.

14:30.360 --> 14:33.980
The ones who may like more customer conversations end up forward deployed.

14:34.220 --> 14:39.860
And what makes this work is that the people selling Clay are the same type of person buying it.

14:39.940 --> 14:43.720
So they deeply understand the customer's pain points and how to fix them.

14:43.860 --> 14:46.040
And that's what makes the loop a revenue engine.

14:46.220 --> 14:50.420
The internal team generates the pipeline, the forward deploy team closes it,

14:50.420 --> 14:52.960
and everything they learn goes straight back in.

14:53.060 --> 14:56.320
It's a flywheel because we like help customers with Clay.

14:56.940 --> 15:02.760
We learn a lot about what they're doing with Clay and then we feed that back into our team so that we can do cool stuff with Clay too.

15:03.000 --> 15:06.600
And because both teams have the same skills, the flywheel actually works.

15:07.400 --> 15:09.780
Because it's like, hey, our CEO is headed to San Francisco.

15:10.360 --> 15:15.800
Who are the 20 best people that we could put around a dinner table with him to like drive the most impact for the business?

15:15.800 --> 15:22.100
We have a live Clay table that at any point you can go in and filter for say SF, Austin, NYC.

15:22.240 --> 15:25.760
And you can build these like super targeted lists.

15:26.020 --> 15:29.620
And then we can actually send the invite on behalf of our founder.

15:29.860 --> 15:35.040
And we can then log responses and like registrants.

15:35.820 --> 15:37.500
And who created this system?

15:37.660 --> 15:39.500
This was created by our GTM engineers.

15:39.920 --> 15:42.420
A forward deploy GTME would build this for our customers.

15:42.820 --> 15:45.500
Clay didn't just coin the term go-to-market engineer.

15:45.500 --> 15:48.460
They became the best example of what it could do.

15:48.800 --> 15:53.900
Two teams, one loop and a $5 billion company to show for it.

15:54.080 --> 15:59.440
And to end the day in true Everett fashion, the team and I headed to his favorite pub.

15:59.920 --> 16:00.700
All right, everybody.

16:00.840 --> 16:02.580
Well, that was our day inside of Clay.

16:02.880 --> 16:06.400
A whole day spent with Everett, their head of go-to-market engineering.

16:06.620 --> 16:08.460
Thanks so much for hanging out with Clay today.

16:08.660 --> 16:14.080
Hope you guys learned about GTM engineering and also what it's like to work at a growing company in New York City.

